e monumental allegorical poem *Roman de la Rose* around 1269-1278, profoundly shaping medieval vernacular literature and intellectual discourse across Western Europe. Born in Meung-sur-Loire, likely in the mid-13th century, his continuation of the *Roman* transformed Guillaume de Lorris's earlier courtly romance into a vast, encyclopedic work that critically examined societal norms, scholastic philosophy, and the complexities of human nature, making it one of the most widely read and influential texts of the late Middle Ages.

His approximately 18,000-line addition to the *Roman de la Rose* introduced a starkly different philosophical tone from Lorris's original, infusing the narrative with elements of naturalism, cynicism, and extensive satire. Jean de Meun employed characters like Reason, Friend, and Genius to debate topics ranging from love and marriage to the nature of nobility, the role of women, and the abuses of the clergy. Drawing heavily on classical sources such as Ovid, Boethius, and Alan of Lille, he synthesized a vast array of contemporary knowledge, from astronomy and alchemy to rhetoric and psychology, presenting a sophisticated, often biting, critique of courtly love and conventional morality.

The expanded *Roman de la Rose* became a cornerstone of French literary tradition, sparking intense intellectual and moral debates for over a century, most notably the "Querelle de la Rose" in the early 15th century concerning its controversial portrayal of women. Its influence extended far beyond France, inspiring writers like Geoffrey Chaucer, who translated parts of it into Middle English, and shaping the allegorical tradition in European literature. The work served as both a mirror and a lamp for its age, reflecting the burgeoning intellectual currents of the Parisian universities and illuminating new pathways for vernacular expression."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-20T08:16:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44700764411", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1240, Meung-sur-Loire"}, {"title": "Literary traditions and narrative storytelling", "type": "Idea", "year": "1240, Meung-sur-Loire"}], "newValue": ["The earlier unfinished work *Roman de la Rose* by Guillaume de Lorris provided a narrative framework awaiting completion and philosophical expansion.", "The intellectual ferment of 13th-century Parisian universities fostered a climate ripe for encyclopedic knowledge and scholastic debate in vernacular literature.", "The growing demand for sophisticated allegorical poetry offered an opportunity for authors to comment on societal and philosophical issues through popular literary forms."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-20T08:16:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44700764411", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Created literary works of enduring influence", "type": "Idea", "year": "1304"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1304"}], "newValue": ["Jean de Meun's continuation of the *Roman 